# Retrieval, end to end, against the real embedding model.
#
# Every other script here avoids embedding. This one runs the whole path —
# tokenizer, ONNX session, pooling, normalization, cosine ranking — over a
# vault that is deliberately mixed: ops runbooks, a policy note, a design doc
# with nested headings, Go, Python, and YAML. Each query is worded to share no
# distinctive term with the chunk it should find, so a keyword match would
# score zero on all of them.
#
# The assertions name the breadcrumb, not just the file. Two of them point
# inside a file with more than one symbol or section, so a match proves the
# chunker and the ranking agreed on *which part* answers the query — the thing
# file-level assertions cannot distinguish.
#
# What this does NOT do is measure quality, and it should not be extended in
# that direction. Both known silent-degradation bugs for this checkpoint —
# mean-pooling instead of CLS, and dropping the query prefix — were run against
# an earlier version of this vault as negative controls, and both still ranked
# correctly; mean pooling in fact scored better. Top-1 over a handful of short
# documents is too coarse to see the difference. What it catches is a pipeline
# broken outright. The representation itself is pinned by exact assertions in
# pkg/embed, which need no ranking judgement. To compare checkpoints, use the
# benchmark in bench_test.go, which reports a score rather than a verdict.
#
# Skipped where the model is absent, so CI stays offline and a contributor who
# has run `semantic init` gets the check for free.

[!embed] skip 'needs the embedding model — run: semantic init'

env SEMANTIC_CACHE_DIR=$WORK/cache
env SEMANTIC_MODEL_DIR=$REAL_MODEL_DIR
env SEMANTIC_ORT_LIB=$REAL_ORT_LIB

semantic index
stdout 'indexed 8 files'

# -n 1 is the assertion: one hit is printed, so a match proves rank 1 rather
# than presence somewhere down the list.

# Markdown, and the nearest distractor is disk.md — both describe a degraded
# machine. "take forever to load" ↔ "ninety-fifth percentile past two seconds".
semantic search --no-reindex -n 1 'pages take forever to load but nothing is broken'
stdout '^runbooks/latency\.md'

# "rate limit" ↔ "a fixed number of calls per minute".
semantic search --no-reindex -n 1 'a customer is hitting the rate limit'
stdout '^runbooks/throttling\.md'

# A different register from the runbooks — policy prose rather than
# instructions. "how long do we keep" ↔ "live for seven years after".
semantic search --no-reindex -n 1 'how long do we keep customer records'
stdout '^retention\.md'

# A heading-tree chunk, and the breadcrumb is asserted: storage.md has two
# sections, and only one answers this.
semantic search --no-reindex -n 1 'why did we pick one big table over many small ones'
stdout 'Why one wide table › The shape we chose'

# Go, by doc comment and signature — the body is never embedded. worker.go
# holds two functions, so the breadcrumb proves the right symbol won.
# "drain in-flight requests" ↔ "waits for the ones already running to finish".
semantic search --no-reindex -n 1 'gracefully drain in-flight requests on shutdown'
stdout 'worker › Drain'

# Python, by docstring, and again the file holds two functions.
# "numbers or dates" ↔ "numbers, dates, or free text".
semantic search --no-reindex -n 1 'guess whether a column holds numbers or dates'
stdout 'etl\.py.*infer_types'

# YAML, which chunks by document rather than by symbol.
semantic search --no-reindex -n 1 'how much memory the container is allowed'
stdout '^deploy\.yaml'

# A recorded limitation, not an oversight: no query here claims worker.Backoff.
# Four phrasings of what it does — "exponential delay between retries",
# "doubling the delay after each failure", "how long to pause before trying a
# failed request again", "spacing out repeated attempts" — all rank a markdown
# chunk above it, and two of them do not put it in the top two at all. The
# short doc comment on a symbol competes badly against a paragraph of prose.
# Written down rather than papered over with an easier vault, because it is the
# kind of thing a future checkpoint should be measured on.

-- runbooks/latency.md --
# Responses have gotten slow

Nothing is returning an error, but the ninety-fifth percentile has climbed past
two seconds. Start at the database: a query that lost its index will do this
long before it starts failing. Check the slow query log for the window where
the curve bent upward.

-- runbooks/throttling.md --
# Clients are being rejected with 429

Each key is allowed a fixed number of calls per minute, and a caller that
exceeds it gets refused until the window rolls over. A single noisy integration
is the common cause. Raise its ceiling deliberately rather than lifting the
global one.

-- runbooks/disk.md --
# The volume is full

Writes begin failing once the underlying block device has no room left. The
usual cause is log rotation that stopped running, not real growth in stored
data. Truncate the oldest archives first, then fix the rotation job.

-- retention.md --
# How long records are kept

Account records live for seven years after the final invoice, which is what the
tax authority requires. Request logs are discarded after ninety days. A person
who asks to be forgotten has the account row anonymized rather than deleted,
because the invoice history has to survive them.

-- design/storage.md --
# Why one wide table

## Contents

- The shape we chose
- What it costs

## The shape we chose

Every event lands in a single append-only table keyed by tenant and time.
Splitting per tenant was considered and rejected: the operational burden of
thousands of small objects outweighed the isolation it would have bought.

## What it costs

A tenant reading its own history scans rows belonging to others and discards
them. That is acceptable while the largest tenant stays under a few percent of
total volume, and it stops being acceptable after.

-- worker.go --
package worker

import "time"

// Backoff returns how long to wait before attempt n, doubling each time up to
// a ceiling. Spreading repeated failures out is what keeps a struggling
// dependency from being hammered by everyone retrying in lockstep.
func Backoff(n int) time.Duration { return 0 }

// Drain stops accepting new items and waits for the ones already running to
// finish, so a shutdown does not abandon work halfway through.
func Drain(timeout time.Duration) error { return nil }

-- etl.py --
